# Introduction

## Background

This document contains an automated report of a health economic decision analysis model intended to estimate the **cost-effectiveness** of complex digital interventions for monitoring electronic Patient-Reported Outcome Measures (**ePROMs**) in cancer care. This model's structure and parameters were sourced from a Systematic Literature Review (**SLR**) of papers related to the impact of ePROM-integrated interventions on:

-   Health Resource Use (**HRU**)

-   Intervention-related healthcare costs

-   Generic health outcomes, usually measured in Quality-Adjusted Life Years ([QALYs](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/quality-adjusted-life-year-qaly/ "What are QALYs and why are they important in health economics? Click the link to find out more."))

**SLR preprint:** <https://preprints.jmir.org/preprint/100505>

## What is ePROM monitoring?

ePROM monitoring interventions can be considered [complex](https://www.bmj.com/content/374/bmj.n2061 "UK Medical Research Council's definition of complex interventions.") due to the number of components and stakeholders involved in their implementation and delivery. While there are many types of ePROM monitoring strategies, *Figure 1* shows a simplified version of how they can work.

