## Base Case Results

The main **base case** results are presented in *Table 2*. A low Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio ([ICER](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/incremental-cost-effectiveness-ratio-icer/ "Definition of an ICER.")) below the Willingness-to-Pay ([WTP](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/cost-effectiveness-threshold/ "Definition of cost-effectiveness thresholds.")) threshold and a positive and Incremental Net Monetary Benefit ([INMB](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/net-monetary-benefit/ "Definition of net monetary benefit.")) indicate that ePROM monitoring is a cost-effective strategy relative to usual care. Conversely, ICER\>WTP and INMB\<0 indicate that ePROM monitoring is *not* a cost-effective strategy. *Figures* *4* and *5* present the survival analysis and cumulative event curves (per population in each strategy), respectively.

The base case results **do not** include any measures of uncertainty (i.e., *How confident are we that our base case results are correct?*). These are covered by the Probabilistic and One-Way Sensitivity Analysis sections.

