## Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis

*Figure 5* presents the Cost-Effectiveness Plane ([CEP](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/cost-effectiveness-plane/ "Cost-effectiveness plane definition.")). Each dot represents a result for a single random model iteration (differences in costs & QALYs, ePROM - Usual Care). On the right-side y-axis, *Figure 6* plots the percentages of model iterations falling below a given WTP threshold to determine the *probability that ePROMs are a cost-effective strategy* at any given WTP level; this is referred to as a Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curve ([CEAC](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/cost-effectiveness-acceptability-curve/ "Definition of a CEAC.")). On the left-side y-axis, *Figure 6* displays the results of the Value-of-Information ([VOI](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/value-of-information-analysis-voi/ "Value of Information")) analysis as the Expected Value of Information ([EVPI](https://www.yhec.co.uk/glossary-term/expected-value-of-perfect-information-evpi/ "Expected Value of Perfect Information")) at each WTP Threshold; this shows the cost of making the wrong decision and the value of reducing uncertainty in future research.

